WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:07.680
When we're looking at data sources, the easiest&nbsp;
first place to start is existing data sets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.160
Conceptually, the easiest way to&nbsp;
do this is with in-house data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:16.400
also in terms of existing data, there's&nbsp;
open data and there's third-party data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:20.080
I'll talk about the pros and&nbsp;
cons of each of these briefly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:26.880
In-house data can be fast and easy because it's&nbsp;
already there. It might be in the proper format&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:32.560
and, if you're lucky, it's documented properly.&nbsp;
On the other hand, in-house data may not be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:38.480
subject to the kind of quality control that data&nbsp;
that's intended for other people might have. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:43.840
there might be restrictions or policies on the&nbsp;
data use even within a particular organization.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:50.160
So, let's look at some of the pros and cons of&nbsp;
in-house data. The first pro is potentially it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:55.760
very quick, easy, and should be free because&nbsp;
you're within the same organization. If you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:59.840
lucky, there's standardized formatting&nbsp;
that the organization has instituted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:06.160
rules or policies about this is the way we do it.&nbsp;
Also, if you're very lucky, the original team that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:11.600
gathered the data and processed it should still&nbsp;
be available. Makes your life very easy when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:17.360
want to answer questions and get insights.&nbsp;
Sometimes, identifiers might be available,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:21.920
and what that means is you might actually know&nbsp;
the identity of each respondent or case in it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:28.320
There are, however, some cons. First off&nbsp;
is the data you need simply may not exist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:35.520
It may be the organization never gathered that&nbsp;
data. Also, the documentation may be inadequate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:40.400
Often when organizations are gathering&nbsp;
their own data, they get it together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:44.640
they use it for what they need to, and then&nbsp;
they kind of store it off to the side but it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:51.120
hard to tell what's what. And also, the quality&nbsp;
of the data may be uncertain. You may not know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:56.240
about response biases or errors in coding and that&nbsp;
makes it a little harder to work with it reliably.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:02.880
An alternative to in-house data is open data.&nbsp;
This prepared data that's freely available.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:07.840
Usually, it's government data but&nbsp;
there's also some open corporate data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:11.680
And there's a move towards more&nbsp;
and more open scientific data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:18.480
Now the pros here are that you can get enormous&nbsp;
data sets. You could get terabytes, possibly even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:24.000
petabytes, that are worth millions of dollars.&nbsp;
You can get a very wide range of topics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:30.400
You can get them covering various historical times&nbsp;
and trends, and the data from open data is often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:36.320
well formatted and well documented. The cons of&nbsp;
open data are that you potentially have biased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:41.920
samples, in that the samples may be restricted to&nbsp;
a particular geographic area or maybe restricted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:46.400
only to people that have internet access, and&nbsp;
those can create problems with interpretation.

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:53.040
Also, the meaning of the variables may not be&nbsp;
clear. You don't have the opportunity to talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:57.280
with the people who gathered the data and it's&nbsp;
sometimes hard to understand why or how they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:02.880
defined a particular variable the way they&nbsp;
did. Also, in some situations open data may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:08.480
stipulate that you need to share your analyses.&nbsp;
That open data is there for open analytics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:13.200
That might be an issue and something you&nbsp;
need to look at carefully before you dive in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:19.440
Finally, openness and data can conflict with the&nbsp;
needs of privacy and confidentiality in data.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:25.600
Finally, there's third-party data, or what&nbsp;
we call data as a service, DaaS. These people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:30.640
are also called data brokers and you can get a&nbsp;
huge amount of data on a large range of topics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:35.440
Also, many of these data brokers&nbsp;
will also process the data to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:38.560
certain kinds of inferences for you&nbsp;
which can save you a lot of time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:43.840
So, the pros of third-party data is that&nbsp;
they can save you a lot of time and effort.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:49.680
They often give you individual-level data.&nbsp;
And you can get summaries and inferences.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:56.480
On the other hand, third-party data can be&nbsp;
very, very expensive. Also, it may still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:01.440
require validation. You still need to double&nbsp;
check that it means what you think it means.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:07.760
And probably most significantly, third-party&nbsp;
data is often very distasteful to many people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:14.960
So, our conclusion. Exercise care in interpreting&nbsp;
data because there's always the possibility of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:19.440
implicate biases or simply because you weren't&nbsp;
there when it got gathered, it may not mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:25.120
exactly what you think it means. That being&nbsp;
said, you can get enormous amounts of information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:34.320
and you can save yourself a lot of time in your&nbsp;
data science projects by using existing data sets.

